68
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:44,400
Now, let's step into the theory of CT.

69
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:46,560
What I'll give you here is a general

70
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,920
overview, much of which should feel like

71
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:53,440
a review from our earlier classes.

72
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:55,120
The basic idea is this. CT uses X-ray

73
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,680
projections taken from many different

74
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:59,680
angles around the body.

75
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:01,840
As the X-rays pass through, they are

76
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,360
attenuated. That means they are weakened

77
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,760
depending on the different properties of

78
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:07,920
the tissues they pass through. Dense

79
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:10,879
tissues like bone attenuate more while

80
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:12,720
soft tissues attenuate less.

81
00:03:10,879 --> 00:03:14,879
By collecting all these projections from

82
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:16,640
multiple views, we build up enough

83
00:03:14,879 --> 00:03:18,879
information to start reconstructing

84
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:22,920
what's inside the body. That's the

85
00:03:18,879 --> 00:03:22,920
foundation of computed tomography.

86
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:29,200
So, let's look a little closer at the

87
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:30,879
general theory of CT.

88
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,799
When X-rays pass through the body,

89
00:03:30,879 --> 00:03:35,280
different tissues attenuate the beam in

90
00:03:32,799 --> 00:03:37,519
different ways. For example, bone blocks

91
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:39,840
attenuate X-rays much more strongly than

92
00:03:37,519 --> 00:03:41,440
muscle or soft tissue. This difference

93
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:44,400
is exactly what gives us useful

94
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:46,159
information.

95
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,080
Now the challenge is that each X-ray

96
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:50,080
image we take is really just a 2D

97
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:52,640
projection. You can see that in the

98
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:54,959
picture on the left. If we only had this

99
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:56,879
single projection, we would miss most of

100
00:03:54,959 --> 00:03:59,760
the information about what lies behind

101
00:03:56,879 --> 00:04:02,879
or to the side of the structures.

102
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:04,560
So what do we do? We take projections

103
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:06,640
from many different angles like the

104
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:08,319
images you see in the middle. Each

105
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,319
projection adds a different piece of

106
00:04:08,319 --> 00:04:12,480
information.

107
00:04:10,319 --> 00:04:14,239
Then comes the key step. We solve what's

108
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:16,160
called an inverse problem.

109
00:04:14,239 --> 00:04:18,799
Mathematically, this is done using the

110
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:20,639
radon transform and its inverse. By

111
00:04:18,799 --> 00:04:23,199
combining all of those projections

112
00:04:20,639 --> 00:04:25,280
through the inverse radon transform, we

113
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:27,520
can reconstruct a detailed image of the

114
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:29,440
body's interior.

115
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:31,360
That's what you see on the right, a 3D

116
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:32,800
CT reconstruction where bones and

117
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:34,639
tissues are highlighted in different

118
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,720
colors.

119
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:39,040
In this class to keep things simpler, we

120
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:41,280
will mostly work with 2D imaging cases

121
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,600
when applying the radon transform in mat

122
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:46,160
lab. But remember the same principle

123
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:49,160
extends to full 3DCT in clinical

124
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,160
practice.

125
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:54,960
Now let's talk about the resolution of

126
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,040
CT reconstruction.

127
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:58,880
Two key factors determine how sharp or

128
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,280
detailed your reconstructed image will

129
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:02,960
be.

130
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,040
First the number of rays or the number

131
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:07,520
of X-rays passing through the object at

132
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:10,320
each angle. This directly affects the

133
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:12,400
radial component of spatial resolution.

134
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:14,639
That is how finely we can distinguish

135
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:16,560
details along a line from the center

136
00:05:14,639 --> 00:05:18,160
outward.

137
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:19,600
Second, the number of views or the

138
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,919
number of different angles is often

139
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,080
denoted by theta. This controls the

140
00:05:21,919 --> 00:05:26,160
circumferential component of resolution.

141
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:28,639
How well we can distinguish details as

142
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:31,120
we go around the circle.

143
00:05:28,639 --> 00:05:33,120
On the cartoon CT slice shown here, you

144
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:35,360
can see both components. The radial

145
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:37,280
resolution going outward from the center

146
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:39,840
and the circumferential resolution

147
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:42,080
wrapping around the image.

148
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:43,840
So to summarize, the more rays you use

149
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:45,199
and the more views you collect, the

150
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,680
higher the resolution of your

151
00:05:45,199 --> 00:05:49,680
reconstruction. On the other hand, using

152
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:54,120
fewer rays or fewer views leads to

153
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:54,120
blurrier images with less detail.

154
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:58,960
Now let's talk about the radon

155
00:05:57,199 --> 00:06:01,280
transform.

156
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:03,759
The radon transform is essentially a way

157
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:07,120
to represent X-ray projection data and

158
00:06:03,759 --> 00:06:09,440
the result is something called a synog.

159
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:11,600
A synog is just an image that shows how

160
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:13,520
the projections vary as we rotate around

161
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,199
the object.

162
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,880
This representation also tells us

163
00:06:15,199 --> 00:06:19,360
something about the characteristics of

164
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:21,120
the sample. For example, objects that

165
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:22,800
are closer to the center of the field of

166
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:25,440
view show up as regions of higher

167
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:26,960
amplitude in the synog.

168
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:29,520
Here you can see the mathematical

169
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:31,360
definition of the radon transform. Don't

170
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:33,120
worry about memorizing the equation.

171
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:35,440
What matters is understanding that it

172
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:37,440
describes how the function f of x and y

173
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:40,800
our object is projected along different

174
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:42,880
angles which we denote by theta.

175
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:45,280
On the left you see the same setup we

176
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:47,840
discussed earlier with xrays passing

177
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:51,039
through the object at angle theta. And

178
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:52,880
on the right you see an example syog.

179
00:06:51,039 --> 00:06:54,720
Notice the bright wavy line that

180
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:57,440
corresponds to the yellow circular

181
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:59,360
object in the field of view.

182
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:01,520
Because it's closer to the center, its

183
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:04,080
contribution to the synog has a higher

184
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,479
amplitude and it traces this curve as

185
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:08,160
the angle changes.

186
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:10,000
So the cyoggram is the bridge between

187
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:12,000
the raw projection data we collect and

188
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:15,000
the reconstructed CT image we want to

189
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
build.

190
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:20,400
So far we've looked at the radon

191
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:22,800
transform. But to actually reconstruct

192
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:25,039
an image, we also need the inverse radon

193
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:28,160
transform. This process relies on

194
00:07:25,039 --> 00:07:30,800
something called the fier slice theorem.

195
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:32,400
Here's the idea in simpler terms. If we

196
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:34,319
take the one-dimensional forier

197
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:36,400
transform of a radon transform

198
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:38,639
projection profile at a specific angle

199
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:40,560
fi, that result is the same as taking

200
00:07:38,639 --> 00:07:42,400
the two-dimensional forier transform of

201
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:45,039
the object and looking along a line at

202
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,560
that same angle fi.

203
00:07:45,039 --> 00:07:48,560
That's powerful because it means each

204
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:51,440
projection we acquire gives us a slice

205
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:53,759
or a line through the object's 2D forier

206
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:55,919
transform. When we combine all these

207
00:07:53,759 --> 00:07:57,759
slices from projections taken at many

208
00:07:55,919 --> 00:08:00,240
different angles, we can fill in the

209
00:07:57,759 --> 00:08:02,000
complete 2D forier transform of the

210
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:04,479
object.

211
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,560
And once we have the full 2D forier

212
00:08:04,479 --> 00:08:09,120
transform, we can simply apply the

213
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:12,479
inverse two-dimensional forier transform

214
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:14,400
to reconstruct the original image.

215
00:08:12,479 --> 00:08:16,960
The diagram here shows this step by

216
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:19,599
step. Each projection corresponds to one

217
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:20,960
line in forier space. Change the angle

218
00:08:19,599 --> 00:08:22,639
and you get another line and then

219
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:24,400
another.

220
00:08:22,639 --> 00:08:26,160
As we add up all these lines at

221
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:28,960
different angles, the forier space is

222
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:31,199
gradually filled. Finally, applying the

223
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:36,039
inverse forier transform brings us back

224
00:08:31,199 --> 00:08:36,039
to a reconstructed image in real space.

225
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:41,599
The next key idea is back projection,

226
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:44,880
which is the standard method for

227
00:08:41,599 --> 00:08:46,800
reconstructing CT slices.

228
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:48,880
Here's how it works. We start with the

229
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:51,360
synog, which contains all the projection

230
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:53,760
data. For each projection, we take its

231
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:55,680
information and back project it. That

232
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:57,519
means we spread that projection back

233
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:59,920
across the image space at the angle

234
00:08:57,519 --> 00:09:02,000
where it was acquired.

235
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:04,240
Let's look at this cartoon example.

236
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,120
Imagine we have a large oval and inside

237
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:09,920
it are two smaller red ovals. If we take

238
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:12,000
a projection from the top down, the syog

239
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:14,959
shows two bright spots that correspond

240
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,560
to those red ovals. When we back project

241
00:09:14,959 --> 00:09:19,760
this data, we get a blurry

242
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:22,160
reconstruction along that one angle.

243
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:24,240
Now let's do the same from a side view.

244
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:26,320
Again, the synogram shows two high

245
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:28,320
amplitude areas. And when we back

246
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:30,399
project that projection, we get another

247
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,800
blurry reconstruction, but this time

248
00:09:30,399 --> 00:09:34,160
along the side angle.

249
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:36,560
At this point, we have two back

250
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:38,880
projections. When we combine them, the

251
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:41,120
overlap starts to suggest the locations

252
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:43,360
of the two smaller ovals inside the

253
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:45,440
larger one. Of course, with only two

254
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:47,120
projections, the result is quite rough.

255
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:48,959
In this case, it looks more like two

256
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:50,800
squares.

257
00:09:48,959 --> 00:09:53,040
But as we add more and more projections

258
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:55,120
from many different angles, the combined

259
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:57,519
back projections gradually approximate

260
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:59,200
the true shapes. With enough views, the

261
00:09:57,519 --> 00:10:02,920
reconstruction recovers the original

262
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:02,920
sample very accurately.

263
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,080
Now let's move from simple back

264
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:10,399
projection to filtered back projection

265
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:12,399
or FBP.

266
00:10:10,399 --> 00:10:14,079
As we saw in the last example, back

267
00:10:12,399 --> 00:10:16,959
projection is the standard way of

268
00:10:14,079 --> 00:10:19,680
reconstructing CT slices. We take the

269
00:10:16,959 --> 00:10:21,200
synog back project each view and combine

270
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:23,440
them.

271
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:25,440
But here's the problem. If we simply use

272
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:27,920
unfiltered back projection, the

273
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:29,760
resulting image often looks blurry.

274
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:32,959
That's because the projections overlap

275
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:35,040
in a way that smears out the details.

276
00:10:32,959 --> 00:10:37,839
To fix this, we apply a filter in the

277
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:39,760
syog space before back projecting.

278
00:10:37,839 --> 00:10:41,519
Filtering helps sharpen the edges and

279
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:43,920
preserve the fine details that would

280
00:10:41,519 --> 00:10:46,320
otherwise be lost.

281
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:47,839
Look at the images here. In the top row,

282
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:50,000
we have a synogram and its back

283
00:10:47,839 --> 00:10:52,399
projected image. Notice how the back

284
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:56,000
projection produces just a fuzzy grayish

285
00:10:52,399 --> 00:10:58,079
blob with poorly defined boundaries.

286
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,839
Now, in the bottom row, the synogram has

287
00:10:58,079 --> 00:11:01,440
first been filtered. After

288
00:10:59,839 --> 00:11:02,880
reconstruction, the image is much

289
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:05,120
clearer. You can actually see the

290
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:07,279
circular structure with sharp edges and

291
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:10,240
even a smaller circle inside it, which

292
00:11:07,279 --> 00:11:12,320
could represent something like a tumor.

293
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:14,079
On the right side, you see examples of

294
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:16,720
different filters that can be applied

295
00:11:14,079 --> 00:11:19,519
such as the Ramlac filter, the Sheplogan

296
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:21,360
filter, and the Hamming filter.

297
00:11:19,519 --> 00:11:23,839
Each of these adjust the frequency

298
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:25,760
content of the syog differently, but

299
00:11:23,839 --> 00:11:28,079
they all serve the same purpose to

300
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:30,880
enhance contrast and reduce blurring in

301
00:11:28,079 --> 00:11:33,279
the final reconstructed image.

302
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:35,360
So in summary, filtered back projection

303
00:11:33,279 --> 00:11:37,680
is one of the most widely used methods

304
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:39,680
in CT because it gives us images that

305
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:43,160
are both accurate and sharp enough for

306
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:43,160
clinical use.

307
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:48,399
Here's something pretty cool to look at.

308
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:50,240
This is filtered back projection in

309
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:52,000
progress.

310
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:54,800
What you're seeing here starts with the

311
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,560
original synog. Then as the algorithm

312
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:58,079
processes each view, the back

313
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,560
projections are gradually added

314
00:11:58,079 --> 00:12:02,560
together. Step by step, the image begins

315
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:03,760
to take shape.

316
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:06,320
It's almost like watching the

317
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:08,240
reconstruction unfold in real time. As

318
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:10,240
more and more views are included, the

319
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:12,480
overlapping lines come together and the

320
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:15,760
original phantom or the sample image we

321
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:18,320
started with slowly reappears.

322
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:20,720
This gives you a visual sense of how CT

323
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:22,320
actually builds up an image, not from a

324
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:24,480
single snapshot, but from many

325
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:27,800
projections combined through filtered

326
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:27,800
back projection.

327
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:33,839
Now, how do we use mat lab to

328
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:35,839
demonstrate these CT principles? Mat Lab

329
00:12:33,839 --> 00:12:38,880
gives us two distinct simulation paths,

330
00:12:35,839 --> 00:12:41,680
parallel beam CT and fan beam CT. We'll

331
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:43,440
explore both step by step.

332
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:45,440
For our demonstrations, we'll work with

333
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:47,680
three simple phantoms that is test

334
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:49,600
images used to evaluate reconstruction

335
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:51,360
methods.

336
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:53,440
Square and square phantom, a small

337
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:55,519
bright square centered inside a larger

338
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:57,680
dark square.

339
00:12:55,519 --> 00:12:59,600
Circle phantom with a nodule, a large

340
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:01,360
gray circle with a small bright circle

341
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:03,519
near the edge. Think of that dot as a

342
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:05,600
mock tumor.

343
00:13:03,519 --> 00:13:07,839
Sheplogan phantom, a standard test

344
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:10,240
object that comes with mat lab. It's

345
00:13:07,839 --> 00:13:12,720
widely used in our field so researchers

346
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:14,959
can compare results fairly. If two

347
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:16,959
groups reconstruct the same Shep Logan

348
00:13:14,959 --> 00:13:18,800
phantom, their images are directly

349
00:13:16,959 --> 00:13:20,880
comparable.

350
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:23,040
We'll first apply parallel beam tools

351
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:24,800
and then switch to fan beam tools so you

352
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:28,040
can see how the algorithms behave on the

353
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:28,040
same phantoms.

354
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:35,120
Let's begin with parallel beam CT

355
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:37,440
in mat lab. The main tool for simulating

356
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:39,600
this is the radon transform function

357
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:42,000
simply called raidon. This function

358
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:43,760
takes an image our phantom and generates

359
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,320
its parallel beam projections at

360
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:48,720
different rotation angles.

361
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:50,720
In a parallel beam setup, the X-ray

362
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:53,040
beams are arranged so that they travel

363
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:54,480
in perfectly parallel lines as they pass

364
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:57,120
through the object and reach the

365
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:59,199
detector. Each projection corresponds to

366
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:02,320
summing the values of the image pixels

367
00:13:59,199 --> 00:14:04,480
along those parallel paths.

368
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:06,639
So when you hear me say parallel beam

369
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:09,120
CT, think of it as collecting straight

370
00:14:06,639 --> 00:14:11,120
evenly spaced rays rotating around the

371
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:14,240
object and storing all of that

372
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:18,440
information in a synog. Mat Lab's radon

373
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:18,440
function gives us exactly that.

374
00:14:19,839 --> 00:14:23,839
Now let's go deeper into how Mat Lab's

375
00:14:21,839 --> 00:14:25,519
radon function works for parallel beam

376
00:14:23,839 --> 00:14:27,519
CT.

377
00:14:25,519 --> 00:14:30,320
Think of the simulated beams as rays

378
00:14:27,519 --> 00:14:32,240
spaced one pixel apart. These beams are

379
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:34,160
projected from the source, travel in

380
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:36,560
straight parallel lines, pass through

381
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:38,800
the object, and arrive at the detector

382
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:40,639
on the other side.

383
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:42,720
In practice, both the beams and the

384
00:14:40,639 --> 00:14:44,880
detector array rotate together around

385
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:46,639
the center of the image. The amount of

386
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:48,639
rotation is controlled by the angle

387
00:14:46,639 --> 00:14:50,639
theta which we provide as an input to

388
00:14:48,639 --> 00:14:52,560
the function. So by sweeping through

389
00:14:50,639 --> 00:14:54,880
many angles, we gather projections from

390
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:57,279
all around the object.

391
00:14:54,880 --> 00:15:00,800
The mat lab function looks like this.

392
00:14:57,279 --> 00:15:03,760
rxp= rate and i theta. Here's what each

393
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:05,760
part means. R is the result. It contains

394
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:08,399
the projection values essentially the

395
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:10,480
amplitudes of the rays.

396
00:15:08,399 --> 00:15:12,720
XP gives the detector positions if you

397
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:15,279
need to keep track of them. I is simply

398
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:16,959
your input image or phantom. Theta is

399
00:15:15,279 --> 00:15:19,440
the set of rotation angles you want to

400
00:15:16,959 --> 00:15:21,360
use.

401
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:22,880
So in one line of code, mat lab takes

402
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,480
your image and simulates what the

403
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:28,600
parallel beam projections would look

404
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:28,600
like at the specified angles.

405
00:15:30,079 --> 00:15:34,880
Let's look at our first mat lab example

406
00:15:32,079 --> 00:15:36,720
for parallel beam CT.

407
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:39,920
If you've downloaded the materials, the

408
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:41,680
code is called ex1 parallel.m

409
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:43,440
you can try running it now or later on

410
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:45,440
your own.

411
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:48,000
Inside the script at line five, there's

412
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:50,240
a variable called p type. By changing

413
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,320
the p type to 1, two, or three, you can

414
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:54,240
switch between the three phantoms. The

415
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:57,759
square, the circle with a small circle

416
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:59,199
inside, or the shepogan phantom.

417
00:15:57,759 --> 00:16:01,120
Here's what happens when you run the

418
00:15:59,199 --> 00:16:02,800
code. On the left, you see the image

419
00:16:01,120 --> 00:16:05,440
domain. In this case, just a simple

420
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:07,120
white square. As the red arrow rotates,

421
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,600
it represents the projection angle,

422
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:12,800
which we call theta. Each angle

423
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:15,519
contributes new data to the synog.

424
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:17,279
On the right, you see the synog domain.

425
00:16:15,519 --> 00:16:19,759
Notice how information is gradually

426
00:16:17,279 --> 00:16:21,920
added as theta changes. For the square,

427
00:16:19,759 --> 00:16:24,639
the synog has a symmetric crisscrossing

428
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:26,720
pattern.

429
00:16:24,639 --> 00:16:28,800
If you switch to the second phantom, the

430
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:31,440
large circle with a small white dot, the

431
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:33,680
synog looks different. The small dot

432
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:35,680
produces a wavy curve in the syog

433
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:40,079
because the detector gets closer to and

434
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:42,320
farther from the dot as it rotates.

435
00:16:40,079 --> 00:16:45,120
Finally, with the Sheplogan phantom, the

436
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:47,040
synog becomes much more complex. There

437
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:48,880
are many structures inside that phantom

438
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:50,639
and each one leaves its signature on the

439
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:52,800
synog.

440
00:16:50,639 --> 00:16:55,040
This complexity is much closer to what

441
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,440
you would see in a real CT scan of the

442
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:59,360
human brain where multiple tissues and

443
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:01,199
structures contribute overlapping

444
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:03,120
patterns.

445
00:17:01,199 --> 00:17:04,720
So the synogram is really just another

446
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:09,000
way of saying here's how the object

447
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:09,000
looks from every possible angle.

448
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:14,799
Now let's try a simple experiment with

449
00:17:12,079 --> 00:17:17,039
the code in the script. There's a

450
00:17:14,799 --> 00:17:19,039
parameter called the theta step. This

451
00:17:17,039 --> 00:17:21,760
sets how finely we sample the rotation

452
00:17:19,039 --> 00:17:24,880
angles. For example, if the step is one,

453
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:27,919
mat lab computes projections at 0 1 2 3

454
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:29,840
degrees and so on all the way to 180.

455
00:17:27,919 --> 00:17:33,360
But if we change the step to five, then

456
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:35,039
mat lab only samples at 0 5 10 15° and

457
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:36,559
so on.

458
00:17:35,039 --> 00:17:40,000
Here's what happens when we make that

459
00:17:36,559 --> 00:17:41,440
change. The synog becomes much coarser.

460
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,840
You can see it here for the square

461
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:46,000
phantom. Instead of smooth continuous

462
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:47,520
curves, the centagram looks blocky

463
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:50,080
because we've skipped over many

464
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:54,000
intermediate angles.

465
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:56,559
What does this mean for reconstruction?

466
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,400
It means lower resolution. If we try to

467
00:17:56,559 --> 00:18:00,640
reconstruct an image from this synogram,

468
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:02,400
the result will appear streaky or blocky

469
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:06,480
because we don't have enough angular

470
00:18:02,400 --> 00:18:08,320
information to fill in the details.

471
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:10,160
So the takeaway is to get a high

472
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:12,080
resolution reconstruction we want a

473
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:14,799
small theta step which gives us many

474
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:17,200
angular views. Fewer angles make the

475
00:18:14,799 --> 00:18:20,200
computation faster but the image quality

476
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:20,200
suffers.

477
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:26,000
Now let's see what happens if we change

478
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:28,080
the maximum theta value.

479
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:31,919
Previously we collected projections from

480
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:35,520
zero all the way up to 180°. But what if

481
00:18:31,919 --> 00:18:37,280
we stop early, say at just 45°?

482
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:39,280
When we do that, the synogram is

483
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:41,280
essentially truncated. Instead of

484
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:43,280
showing information from a full half

485
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:45,440
circle of projections, it only covers a

486
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:47,280
small portion of the angles. In other

487
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:49,039
words, we've only scanned part of the

488
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:53,080
sample.

489
00:18:49,039 --> 00:18:53,080
What does that mean for reconstruction?

490
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:58,400
If we only gather projections from 0 to

491
00:18:55,760 --> 00:19:00,000
45°, then when we try to reconstruct the

492
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:01,919
image, we're missing most of the

493
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,840
information. the result will be

494
00:19:01,919 --> 00:19:06,080
incomplete and inaccurate because the

495
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:08,320
algorithm never saw the object from the

496
00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:10,240
other directions.

497
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:12,000
So limiting the maximum theta is like

498
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:14,000
taking photos of a sculpture but only

499
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,320
from one side. You lose the complete

500
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,080
picture. To get a full and accurate

501
00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:22,360
reconstruction, we need projections

502
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:22,360
across the whole angular range.

503
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,240
So far we've seen how to use the forward

504
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:30,400
radon transform with mat lab's radon

505
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:32,080
function. But to actually reconstruct an

506
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:33,679
image we need the inverse radon

507
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:36,960
transform which in mat lab is

508
00:19:33,679 --> 00:19:39,039
implemented as the function iodon.

509
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:41,360
This function performs back projection

510
00:19:39,039 --> 00:19:43,360
for parallel beam synograms and it can

511
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:45,360
also carry out filtered back projection

512
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:47,919
if we choose.

513
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:51,120
Here's the format of the function. I=

514
00:19:47,919 --> 00:19:53,760
iraen r theta inter filter. I is the

515
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:55,919
reconstructed image the output we want.

516
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:58,320
R is the synogram the input data we've

517
00:19:55,919 --> 00:20:00,480
collected. Theta is the set of rotation

518
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:02,640
angles used.

519
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:04,559
Interp is the interpolation method. This

520
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:06,960
tells mat lab how to fill in values

521
00:20:04,559 --> 00:20:08,640
between samples. The filter is the

522
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:10,640
filter we want to apply for filtered

523
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:12,640
back projection.

524
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:14,720
The last two arguments interpolation and

525
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:17,039
filter are optional. That means you

526
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:19,120
could just write I raid and r theta to

527
00:20:17,039 --> 00:20:20,960
perform simple unfiltered back

528
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:22,640
projection.

529
00:20:20,960 --> 00:20:25,039
But if you include a filter such as

530
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:27,200
Ramlock or Shep Logan, Mat Lab will

531
00:20:25,039 --> 00:20:29,440
automatically implement filtered back

532
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:32,640
projection giving you a sharper and more

533
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:34,640
accurate image.

534
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:36,880
So in short, Radon simulates the

535
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:39,120
projections while Iridon reconstructs

536
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:41,280
the image from those projections.

537
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:45,240
Together they form the foundation of CT

538
00:20:41,280 --> 00:20:45,240
simulation in mat lab.

539
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:50,880
Let's walk through example two, parallel

540
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:53,039
beam back projection.

541
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:54,880
The code for this is called ex2

542
00:20:53,039 --> 00:20:57,600
parallelback.mm.

543
00:20:54,880 --> 00:21:00,080
Inside the script, line 32 runs a normal

544
00:20:57,600 --> 00:21:03,840
back projection with no filter and line

545
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:05,600
35 applies a filtered back projection.

546
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:08,400
In this case, I've included the Hamming

547
00:21:05,600 --> 00:21:10,000
filter as an example. But mat lab also

548
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:13,039
supports other filter types such as

549
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,799
Ramlock, Shep Logan, Cosine, and HAN.

550
00:21:13,039 --> 00:21:16,720
You can even design your own filter and

551
00:21:14,799 --> 00:21:19,600
add it as an input if you want to

552
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:22,000
experiment.

553
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:23,679
Now, let's compare the results. On the

554
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,840
left, you see the reconstruction of a

555
00:21:23,679 --> 00:21:28,320
simple white square using unfiltered

556
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:30,000
back projection. Notice how the image

557
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:32,159
shows streaks in a kind of cross

558
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:36,320
pattern. That's the artifact caused by

559
00:21:32,159 --> 00:21:37,919
overlapping unfiltered projections.

560
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:40,400
On the right, you see the result with

561
00:21:37,919 --> 00:21:42,559
filtered back projection. The square is

562
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:44,880
much cleaner with sharper edges and less

563
00:21:42,559 --> 00:21:46,640
background noise. The filter removes the

564
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:48,400
smearing and helps reveal the actual

565
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:50,320
structure.

566
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:52,480
If you change the phantom, such as using

567
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:54,240
the Shep Logan Phantom, you may notice

568
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:56,559
that different filters give slightly

569
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:58,480
different results. Some filters are

570
00:21:56,559 --> 00:22:01,840
better for enhancing edges, while others

571
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:03,760
help reduce blurriness or noise.

572
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:06,080
This is why in practice, the choice of

573
00:22:03,760 --> 00:22:08,799
filter depends on what details are most

574
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:10,880
important for your application.

575
00:22:08,799 --> 00:22:13,120
So, this example shows us the power of

576
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:15,039
filtering. Without it, back projection

577
00:22:13,120 --> 00:22:17,120
images can be full of artifacts. But

578
00:22:15,039 --> 00:22:21,240
with it, we get reconstructions that are

579
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:21,240
much more useful and accurate.

580
00:22:22,799 --> 00:22:26,640
Now, let's take a closer look at how

581
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:29,520
changing the maximum theta or the theta

582
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:32,080
step affects reconstruction.

583
00:22:29,520 --> 00:22:36,559
By default, our code uses a maximum

584
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:40,880
theta of 180° and a step size of 1°. But

585
00:22:36,559 --> 00:22:42,720
what happens if we adjust those values?

586
00:22:40,880 --> 00:22:46,159
On the left, you see the case where max

587
00:22:42,720 --> 00:22:47,760
theta is 180 and the step is five. Here,

588
00:22:46,159 --> 00:22:49,280
we're still covering the full range of

589
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:51,200
angles, but we're sampling them more

590
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:53,360
coarsely.

591
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:55,520
As a result, the back projected image

592
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:57,360
shows more artifacts. Notice the

593
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:59,600
diagonal streaks in the unfiltered

594
00:22:57,360 --> 00:23:01,440
image. Filtering helps reduce some of

595
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:04,960
these, but the square edges are still

596
00:23:01,440 --> 00:23:06,880
less crisp compared to the default case.

597
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:09,840
On the right, we have max theta reduced

598
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:11,360
to 90° with a step of one. Now, the

599
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:12,880
issue is different. We've cut the

600
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:15,360
angular range in half, so the

601
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:17,360
reconstruction is incomplete. The square

602
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:19,600
looks more rounded at the edges, and you

603
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:21,280
see stronger cross-like artifacts in the

604
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:23,840
background.

605
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:26,000
So, here's the key takeaway. Increasing

606
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:29,440
the step size, fewer sampled angles,

607
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:31,520
makes the reconstructions less defined.

608
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:33,679
Reducing the maximum angle, smaller

609
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:36,480
angular coverage lowers resolution and

610
00:23:33,679 --> 00:23:38,559
produces incomplete images.

611
00:23:36,480 --> 00:23:40,960
In real CT scanning, both of these

612
00:23:38,559 --> 00:23:43,039
parameters matter. More angles mean

613
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:45,760
better images, but also more computation

614
00:23:43,039 --> 00:23:50,280
and radiation dose. Fewer angles save

615
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:50,280
time and dose, but reduce quality.

616
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:56,000
Now, let's turn to the second type of CT

617
00:23:54,240 --> 00:23:58,799
geometry that Mat Lab can simulate,

618
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:01,280
which is fan beam CT.

619
00:23:58,799 --> 00:24:03,840
Unlike parallel beam CT where all the

620
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:06,799
X-ray beams are perfectly parallel, in

621
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:09,120
fan beam CT, the rays spread out from a

622
00:24:06,799 --> 00:24:12,080
single source point forming a fan shape

623
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:14,720
as they pass through the object.

624
00:24:12,080 --> 00:24:16,960
This setup is much closer to how real CT

625
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:18,720
scanners work today because the source

626
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:21,440
rotates around the patient while the

627
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:23,520
rays diverge outward.

628
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:25,520
As I mentioned in class, fan beam

629
00:24:23,520 --> 00:24:27,440
geometry introduces some additional

630
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:29,600
considerations compared to the parallel

631
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:32,480
beam case, but it also gives us more

632
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:34,559
realistic simulations. Mat Lab provides

633
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:36,159
dedicated functions to handle fan beam

634
00:24:34,559 --> 00:24:39,159
CT, which we'll look at in the next

635
00:24:36,159 --> 00:24:39,159
slides.

636
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:44,559
Now, let's look at Mat Lab's fan beam

637
00:24:42,799 --> 00:24:46,559
function.

638
00:24:44,559 --> 00:24:49,120
In this geometry, the X-rays don't

639
00:24:46,559 --> 00:24:51,120
travel in parallel lines like before.

640
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:52,720
Instead, they spread out in a fan shape

641
00:24:51,120 --> 00:24:55,200
from a single point called the beam

642
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:57,440
vertex. You can see that illustrated

643
00:24:55,200 --> 00:25:00,000
here. The source is at the top and the

644
00:24:57,440 --> 00:25:01,760
rays diverge outward passing through the

645
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,279
sample.

646
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:05,200
On the opposite side, we have the

647
00:25:03,279 --> 00:25:07,919
detector array that records how much of

648
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:09,520
the X-rays make it through.

649
00:25:07,919 --> 00:25:11,760
There's also an important parameter here

650
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:13,679
called D. This is the distance from the

651
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:16,240
fan beam vertex, that is the source

652
00:25:13,679 --> 00:25:19,120
position, to the center of rotation of

653
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:20,720
the sample.

654
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:22,720
For something simple like a square

655
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:25,440
phantom, D might just be the length of

656
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:27,200
the diagonal. For a human body, D would

657
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:29,440
be the distance from the source to the

658
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:31,840
center of the patient.

659
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:33,840
As in parallel beam CT, both the source

660
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:35,440
and detector rotate together around the

661
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:38,080
object at different angles, which we

662
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:40,080
denote by theta. By sweeping through all

663
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:41,520
these angles, we collect the fan beam

664
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:43,600
projection data needed for

665
00:25:41,520 --> 00:25:45,840
reconstruction.

666
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:47,919
So the fan beam function in mat lab

667
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:49,600
allows us to simulate this geometry

668
00:25:47,919 --> 00:25:54,279
directly bringing us closer to how

669
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:54,279
modern CT scanners actually operate.

670
00:25:55,840 --> 00:26:00,640
Now let's look at the fan beam function

671
00:25:57,679 --> 00:26:02,720
in mat lab in more detail.

672
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:05,360
This function calculates projection data

673
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:07,760
for a specified fan beam geometry.

674
00:26:05,360 --> 00:26:10,320
Unlike the parallel beam case here the

675
00:26:07,760 --> 00:26:13,279
rotation angles are fixed from 0 to

676
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:15,039
360°.

677
00:26:13,279 --> 00:26:17,919
That means you can't restrict the scan

678
00:26:15,039 --> 00:26:20,799
to only half a circle like 0 to 180 or

679
00:26:17,919 --> 00:26:23,760
just 0 to 45. The fan beam function

680
00:26:20,799 --> 00:26:26,559
always assumes a full rotation.

681
00:26:23,760 --> 00:26:29,279
Here's what the function looks like. F1

682
00:26:26,559 --> 00:26:32,640
sensor POS1 fan rot angles one equals

683
00:26:29,279 --> 00:26:34,159
fan beam PD fan sensor spacing desensor

684
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:36,480
1.

685
00:26:34,159 --> 00:26:38,799
Let's break it down.

686
00:26:36,480 --> 00:26:41,360
F1 is the fan beam projection data. The

687
00:26:38,799 --> 00:26:43,600
main output you need. Sensor pause one

688
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:45,600
gives the positions of the detectors.

689
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:48,640
Fan rot angles one lists the rotation

690
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:51,039
angles used. P is your input image or

691
00:26:48,640 --> 00:26:53,200
phantom. D is the distance from the

692
00:26:51,039 --> 00:26:55,200
source vertex to the object's center of

693
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:57,520
rotation.

694
00:26:55,200 --> 00:26:59,679
Fan sensor spacing is a property name

695
00:26:57,520 --> 00:27:02,720
which tells mat lab you're specifying

696
00:26:59,679 --> 00:27:06,480
the spacing between detectors. Densor

697
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:09,039
one is the actual spacing value.

698
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:10,799
Those last two inputs are optional. You

699
00:27:09,039 --> 00:27:12,720
can run the function with just the image

700
00:27:10,799 --> 00:27:15,039
and the distance and mat lab will use

701
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:16,799
default detector spacing. But if you

702
00:27:15,039 --> 00:27:18,640
want more control say to change the

703
00:27:16,799 --> 00:27:21,679
resolution or field of view, you can

704
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:23,600
specify these additional arguments.

705
00:27:21,679 --> 00:27:26,799
So in short, Fan Beam gives you

706
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:29,120
projection data over a full 360° with

707
00:27:26,799 --> 00:27:32,120
flexibility to adjust sensor spacing if

708
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:32,120
needed.

709
00:27:33,520 --> 00:27:37,520
Now that we've seen how mat lab

710
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:39,360
generates fan beam projections, the next

711
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:41,840
step is to reconstruct an image from

712
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:43,520
them. For that, we use the I fan beam

713
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:45,440
function.

714
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:47,760
Here's what happens under the hood. I

715
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:49,840
fan beam first converts the fan beam

716
00:27:47,760 --> 00:27:52,240
data into equivalent parallel beam

717
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:54,080
projections. Once that's done, it

718
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:56,159
applies the same filtered back

719
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:58,640
projection algorithm we studied earlier

720
00:27:56,159 --> 00:28:01,279
to perform the inverse radon transform

721
00:27:58,640 --> 00:28:04,159
and reconstruct the image.

722
00:28:01,279 --> 00:28:07,279
The function looks like this. I fan 1

723
00:28:04,159 --> 00:28:10,880
equals I fan beam F1D fan sensor spacing

724
00:28:07,279 --> 00:28:13,840
D sensor 1. Breaking it down, Eon 1 is

725
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:16,080
the reconstructed image, the output.

726
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:18,640
F1 is the synogram data produced by the

727
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:22,799
fan beam function. D is the distance

728
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:24,799
from the source to the object's center.

729
00:28:22,799 --> 00:28:26,720
Fan sensor spacing is the property name

730
00:28:24,799 --> 00:28:29,600
that tells Mat Lab you're specifying

731
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:31,919
detector spacing. D sensor one is the

732
00:28:29,600 --> 00:28:33,919
actual spacing value.

733
00:28:31,919 --> 00:28:36,159
The last two inputs are optional. You

734
00:28:33,919 --> 00:28:39,600
can let mat lab use default spacing if

735
00:28:36,159 --> 00:28:41,840
you don't need to adjust it.

736
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:43,919
So just like with parallel beam CT, we

737
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:46,399
have a pair of functions. Fan beam to

738
00:28:43,919 --> 00:28:48,640
simulate the projections and if beam to

739
00:28:46,399 --> 00:28:51,200
reconstruct the image together they

740
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:54,919
allow us to model fan beam CT entirely

741
00:28:51,200 --> 00:28:54,919
within mat lab.

742
00:28:56,240 --> 00:29:00,080
Now let's move to example three.

743
00:28:57,840 --> 00:29:03,039
fanbeamct.

744
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:04,799
If you run the script ex3 fanbeam.mm,

745
00:29:03,039 --> 00:29:08,000
you'll see how mat lab handles this

746
00:29:04,799 --> 00:29:10,799
geometry. Inside the code at line 25,

747
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:12,880
there's a parameter called d.

748
00:29:10,799 --> 00:29:14,880
By convention, d is set to be slightly

749
00:29:12,880 --> 00:29:18,399
larger than half the diagonal length of

750
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:20,640
the image. Then at line 26, the d sensor

751
00:29:18,399 --> 00:29:23,840
value is set to one, which specifies the

752
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:25,840
spacing between detector elements.

753
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:28,720
On the left, you can see the fan beam

754
00:29:25,840 --> 00:29:30,399
syog for a simple square phantom. On the

755
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:33,039
right, you see the reconstruction using

756
00:29:30,399 --> 00:29:35,039
filtered back projection.

757
00:29:33,039 --> 00:29:37,120
The square shape is clear and

758
00:29:35,039 --> 00:29:40,000
recognizable, but you may also notice

759
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:41,840
small dots or speckles near the corners.

760
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:43,360
These are reconstruction artifacts, and

761
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:45,120
if you run this code on your own

762
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:46,880
computer, you'll probably see them more

763
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:49,279
clearly.

764
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:51,039
For more complex phantoms, the synogram

765
00:29:49,279 --> 00:29:54,159
looks similar to what we saw in earlier

766
00:29:51,039 --> 00:29:56,799
examples, but extended over a full 0 to

767
00:29:54,159 --> 00:29:58,880
360°.

768
00:29:56,799 --> 00:30:00,880
The reconstructions in those cases often

769
00:29:58,880 --> 00:30:03,520
show diagonal streaks or artifacts

770
00:30:00,880 --> 00:30:05,039
around the edges. Even so, the important

771
00:30:03,520 --> 00:30:06,960
structural information inside the

772
00:30:05,039 --> 00:30:10,399
phantom like the outlines of shapes

773
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:12,559
remains fairly accurate.

774
00:30:10,399 --> 00:30:14,399
So, Fan Beam CT and Mat Lab gives us

775
00:30:12,559 --> 00:30:16,640
realistic results, but it also shows us

776
00:30:14,399 --> 00:30:18,240
that no reconstruction is perfect.

777
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:20,000
Artifacts are always a part of the

778
00:30:18,240 --> 00:30:22,640
process and learning how to recognize

779
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:25,640
and minimize them is a big part of CT

780
00:30:22,640 --> 00:30:25,640
imaging.

781
00:30:27,279 --> 00:30:31,600
Now let's experiment with sensor spacing

782
00:30:29,200 --> 00:30:33,679
and fan beam CT.

783
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:36,960
In our code, this is the Densor value on

784
00:30:33,679 --> 00:30:40,640
line 26. By default, we set it to one.

785
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:44,240
But what happens if we change it?

786
00:30:40,640 --> 00:30:46,000
On the left, we set D sensor equals 0.5.

787
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:48,640
This means the detectors are closer

788
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:50,399
together, giving us finer sampling. The

789
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:52,320
result is higher resolution. You can

790
00:30:50,399 --> 00:30:54,720
clearly see the square shape with only

791
00:30:52,320 --> 00:30:56,559
minor artifacts.

792
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:58,880
In the middle, we keep the D sensor

793
00:30:56,559 --> 00:31:00,720
equal to one, the default value. The

794
00:30:58,880 --> 00:31:02,240
reconstruction is still decent, but not

795
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:04,399
quite as sharp as when we use the

796
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:07,200
smaller spacing.

797
00:31:04,399 --> 00:31:09,440
On the right, we set D sensor equals 5.

798
00:31:07,200 --> 00:31:11,919
Now, the detector spacing is wide, so we

799
00:31:09,440 --> 00:31:13,760
lose a lot of detail. The reconstruction

800
00:31:11,919 --> 00:31:15,760
looks blurred and distorted. The square

801
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:18,159
shape is almost unrecognizable, more

802
00:31:15,760 --> 00:31:20,399
like a rounded blob.

803
00:31:18,159 --> 00:31:22,960
So the key point here is resolution is

804
00:31:20,399 --> 00:31:25,679
directly related to detector spacing.

805
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:29,200
Smaller spacing means higher resolution.

806
00:31:25,679 --> 00:31:30,960
Larger spacing means lower resolution.

807
00:31:29,200 --> 00:31:32,559
In practice, of course, reducing sensor

808
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:34,720
spacing increases data size and

809
00:31:32,559 --> 00:31:36,799
computational load. But if the spacing

810
00:31:34,720 --> 00:31:39,120
is too large, the resolution drops too

811
00:31:36,799 --> 00:31:40,720
much to be useful. Finding the right

812
00:31:39,120 --> 00:31:44,399
balance is part of designing and

813
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:44,399
operating a CT system.

814
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:49,760
Now let's test what happens when we

815
00:31:47,679 --> 00:31:51,760
change the parameter D in our fan beam

816
00:31:49,760 --> 00:31:53,679
simulation.

817
00:31:51,760 --> 00:31:55,519
Remember D is the distance from the fan

818
00:31:53,679 --> 00:31:58,080
beam source vertex to the center of the

819
00:31:55,519 --> 00:32:01,919
object. In our code, this is set in line

820
00:31:58,080 --> 00:32:03,840
28. The default value is 191, which is

821
00:32:01,919 --> 00:32:05,440
roughly half the diagonal length of the

822
00:32:03,840 --> 00:32:08,080
image.

823
00:32:05,440 --> 00:32:11,279
Here's what happens on the left with D

824
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:13,600
equals 191. Default the reconstruction

825
00:32:11,279 --> 00:32:16,480
looks good. The square phantom is sharp

826
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:18,320
and the resolution is reasonable.

827
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:21,120
In the middle, when we increase D to

828
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:23,360
300, the resolution decreases. The edges

829
00:32:21,120 --> 00:32:27,200
become less clear and faint artifacts

830
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:29,679
start to appear in the background.

831
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:32,080
On the right, with D equals 500, the

832
00:32:29,679 --> 00:32:34,240
